"y = 0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086329 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086329 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e3a733ff-d529-5f11-83d5-26f3283916e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086330 #edb/ref 1080 "y = \\frac{1}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086330 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086330 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "efdb67da-e129-5640-9779-c117b945dab3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086331 #edb/ref 1080 "No horizontal asymptotes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086331 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086331 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b3a2b1a-ef2a-573d-b248-54b8b0775046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086332 #edb/ref 1080 "y = 7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086332 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086332 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3e10cd66-0241-5a85-8954-e10915c1bf02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086333 #edb/ref 1080 "y = \\frac{7}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086333 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186086333 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6907de6e-2574-5efe-8637-7c4f30a018a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087578 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5847" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087578 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{4x^3-x}{3x^3+24}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087578 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb0b899e-af3a-587c-96ae-f1da9e0929dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087578 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^3.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}-x}{3{x}^{3}+24} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ and $\\dfrac{24}{x^3}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(4-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(3+\\frac{24}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{4-0}{3+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = \\dfrac{4}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087579 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5849" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087579 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{3x^2+ 2x}{6x^4-1}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087579 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0d53819b-ca24-549d-98e2-a9b78b49c080" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087579 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^4.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x}{6{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms  $\\dfrac{3}{x^2}, \\dfrac{2}{x^3},$ and $\\dfrac{1}{x^4}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(6-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y = 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087580 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5851" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087580 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x) = \\dfrac{2x^3}{x^2-1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087580 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea2a97a7-fce8-5b4a-a269-57e0ae7984d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087580 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x^2.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y = f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087851 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087851 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087851 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087851 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087851 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087851 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087851 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087851 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186294941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087851 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087851 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087851 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087851 